"Rebases multiple time series to a common reference so proportional change can be compared across different initial levels.\n\nUse when: Series share a meaningful baseline occasion but have different starting magnitudes. The question concerns relative growth rather than absolute quantities.\n\nAvoid when: Baseline values are zero, negative, unstable, or not comparable.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes time. Vertical position encodes value divided by its baseline, commonly multiplied by 100.\n\nBuild: Choose and label a common baseline period. Calculate each valid series relative to its own baseline value. Draw a shared index scale with a prominent 100 reference and endpoint labels.\n\nCheck: Check every series equals 100 at the declared baseline. Verify missing baseline values are handled explicitly. State that index-point changes differ from percentage-point changes in original measures.\n\nPitfalls: Baseline selection can materially change the apparent story. Crossings mean equal relative growth, not equal absolute values; use small multiples if paths become difficult to trace.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic visitor growth at three venues after rebasing each venue’s first month to 100.\n\nAlternatives: Line chart, Seasonal overlay plot, Dumbbell chart."
